  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nIssued 238 PM MDT FRI OCT 2 2026\nA passing shortwave disturbance will move through the area on \nMonday, flattening the ridge. This weak system will provide\ncloud cover, low precipitation chances (5-10%, primarily over \nthe higher terrain along the Nevada border and central Idaho), \nand cooling temperatures by a few degrees (but overall remaining\nwell above normal). As the trough moves out, the upper-level \nridging will build right back in, keeping our temperatures warm \nfor this time of year. Clear skies, dry conditions, and warm \ntemperatures are forecast Tuesday through Thursday. \n\nEarly Friday morning, an upper-level trough will approach the \nPacific Northwest. Temperatures are expected to remain warm\nthrough Friday with cooler temperatures and higher \nprecipitation chances not arriving until the weekend, but \nconfidence on the timing and intensity of this system is low. \nDepending on the intensity and timing, the range of \npossibilities is vast; ranging from some tropical moisture \nmoving into the area to an intense mid-latitude cyclone bringing\nmuch cooler air. For now, uncertainty remains too high to \nmessage anything strongly.",
